7.1.m:
They are all without possessions, yet devoted to sharing;
they are eaters, yet devoid of conduct; they are proclaimed as spirits and the like.
The first creation of Brahmā, the Supreme Deity, is that of the Mahat;
the second is that of the subtle elements, which is called the elemental creation.

having gratified their own father, the Supreme Lord, through tapas,
and having attained his grace with their whole being from the Supreme Deity:
in an earlier kalpa, Rudra created Brahmā and Nārāyaṇa;
in another kalpa again, Brahmā, whose nature is the universe, created Rudra and Viṣṇu;

restrained himself from the creation of offspring and remained with the Rudras.
From then on, that god does not produce auspicious offspring;
with his seed drawn upward, he stood as Sthāṇu until the dissolution of beings.

Having propitiated Brahmā by asceticism, I will quickly become Gaurī.”
Īśvara said:
“In the past, Brahmā attained the state of Brahmā by my grace;
